## 3. The dependency set

{% if node_lib %}A `node-lib` repository declares no rn-forge library: its root `pyproject.toml`
carries only kiln's tooling and the docs group, and its code dependencies are
pnpm's.
{% else %}{% if workspace %}The archetype specification supplies its component-library set.
Every rn-forge distributable depends on `rn-forge-commons`, and each workspace
package declares the full URL itself. The root declares no rn-forge source at
all: a member is published on its own, so its dependency has to be resolvable
from its own metadata.

A `python-lib` repo that *contains* one of these libraries — pykit — renders an
empty required list. The list is config, so that is the mechanism working
rather than an exemption from it.

{% elif lifecycle %}The archetype specification supplies its component-library set. For
`python-tool` that is all three python libraries — `rn-forge-commons` for
runtime-neutral mechanisms, `rn-forge-cli` for the process and command-line
shape, and `rn-forge-tooling` for the machinery of a program that installs
itself, owns files in someone else's repo, or renders templates. All three are
runtime dependencies: a tool's development layer *is* its runtime.

A `python-app` repo takes the first two and stops there. A repo takes the
highest layer it actually needs, and the checker's required list is what makes
that a rule rather than a preference.

{% else %}The archetype specification supplies its component-library set. For
`python-app` that is `rn-forge-commons` — runtime-neutral mechanisms — and
`rn-forge-cli` — the process and command-line shape. Both are runtime
dependencies.

It stops there. `rn-forge-tooling` is the machinery of a program that installs
itself, owns files in someone else's repo, or renders templates; that is a
`python-tool`, and taking the layer here would be a dependency nothing imports.
A repo takes the highest layer it actually needs, and the checker's required
list is what makes that a rule rather than a preference.

{% endif %}The web archetypes add `rn-forge-web` — framework-free inbound HTTP, on commons
alone — beneath `rn-forge-django` or `rn-forge-fastapi`.

rn-forge packages resolve from PyPI; a root declares no index for them. Their
requirements are version ranges declared in `dependencies`, never as
`[tool.uv.sources]` overrides, which do not survive into a built wheel.{% if workspace %} A sibling workspace member is the exception: it may use a range or a pinned URL, may add a `{ workspace = true }` source, and may be named bare in a dependency group.{% endif %} The
floor is kiln's pin for the package and the ceiling is the next breaking
release:

```toml
dependencies = [
  "rn-forge-commons>=0.8.1,<0.9",
{% if not workspace %}  "rn-forge-cli>=0.1.2,<0.2",
{% endif %}{% if lifecycle %}  "rn-forge-tooling>=0.3.1,<0.4",
{% endif %}]
```

The dependency source is config: `git` + ref, which is CI-capable, or a local
`path`, which is not. `kiln doctor`'s `rn-forge-deps` check enforces required,
allowed and pinned-or-ranged, accepts a branch or path source with a warning,
and refuses one in a publish job. A breaking pykit release is
`kiln config upgrade --apply`, not a template change.

{% if not workspace %}Every root outside `python-lib` sets `[tool.uv] no-sources = true`. pykit's
tags sit on different commits, and pykit's own workspace sources would
otherwise make uv resolve a shared dependency once per tag. A repository
scaffolded before kiln did this adds the line by hand.

{% endif %}{% endif %}
kiln is not a dependency. `[kiln] ref` in `config.toml` pins it, and `task` runs
it with `uvx` in its own environment (kiln ADR-0006). A
repository's lock holds none of kiln's dependencies, and kiln's pykit pins never
constrain the repository's.

