{% endif %}{% endif %}## 5. What CI checks, and what it does not

CI runs the pinned kiln read-only: it never runs `kiln apply`
and never writes a generated file. `task validate` runs `kiln doctor` on every
run; `kiln doctor --full` runs where the workflow asks, through `lint` with
the `KILN_DOCTOR_FULL` task variable set. `[ci] doctor_full` chooses
where: pushes to the default branch and dispatch by default, or every run.
Branch protection requires `ci-ok` and nothing else.

{% if release == "pypi" %}`[ci] release = "pypi"` makes each package's release job upload to pypi.org by
trusted publishing, with a PEP 740 attestation for each wheel and sdist, before
it creates the GitHub release, so a published release implies the version is on
PyPI. A dispatch with `testpypi` set uploads every
package to TestPyPI instead and creates no tag or release. Once per repository,
the owner creates the GitHub environments `pypi` (deployment branch `main`) and
`testpypi`, and adds a trusted publisher per package on pypi.org and on
test.pypi.org, naming workflow `ci.yml` and the environment.

{% endif %}{% if docs %}`task validate` proves: {% if not node_lib %}ruff is clean and formatted{% if workspace %} in every package{% endif %}; {% endif %}the archetype's
dependency set is present, allowed and pinned; {% if not node_lib %}the import contracts hold; {% endif %}the
task layout and validate gate are intact; no workflow step invokes a wrapped
